※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A type may not reach itself (4408), because a value of one would have to hold
※ a value of itself and there would be no size that fitted.  **A reference is
※ the indirection that makes it finite**: what a reference occupies is the same
※ whatever it names, so a definition may reach itself through one and a value
※ of it is as big as its other fields and one address.
※
※ That is the list, the tree and the graph -- every shape whose definition says
※ "and another one of me" -- and it is what a reference in a product is for.
※
※ The rule is about the way round and not about the length of it: a chain of
※ definitions with a reference anywhere on it is finite, and one with none on
※ it is not, however many types it passes through.  So `A = b : B` beside
※ `B = a : A` is still refused, and the test beside this one shows it.
※
※ Nothing here makes a *value* of one yet: the language has no syntax for
※ writing a product, which is a question of its own.  What is settled is that
※ the type is a type.

※ The list: one of these, and where the next one is.
type Node = value : u8 ; next : &mut Node

※ The tree, which reaches itself twice and through a reference that does not
※ allow writing.
type Tree = leaf : u8 ; left : &Tree ; right : &Tree

※ Two definitions that reach each other, with a reference on the way round.
type Pair = n : u8 ; other : &Other
type Other = m : u16 ; back : &Pair

※ And a sum, which is nominal for the same reason and reaches itself the same
※ way.
type Chain = nothing : void | more : &Chain

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6
